  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nInverted surface trough will push eastward across the southern\nhalf of the state tonight. Meanwhile a weak backdoor front will\npush across the north during the same time, possibly influencing\nthe surface trough and associated warm front. To start, as the\ntrough moves east, increased northerly winds will be found with\nsome very low RH values in the teens across western portions. A\ncouple hours of near critical fire weather conditions are\npossible as a result through this evening, especially in\nsouthwestern portions. This feature will meet abundant CAPE and\nshear in south central and southeastern portions this evening,\nwith a shear vector favoring discrete supercells. There are\nbrief periods the surface and mix layers are limitedly capped,\nalthough for the most part much of the instability is elevated.\nThere is also a brief period where hodographs would support\nrotating supercells vs splitting cells with weak rotation. Thus\nthere remains a conditional risk for isolated severe storms\nthrough this evening. Main hazards will be hail to golf ball\nsize given the possibility for discrete cells combined with\nmodest to high CAPE and shear, along with winds to 60 mph. LCLs\nlook too high for a tornado threat despite some low STP numbers\nthis evening. Thunderstorms will likely not be surface based\nwhich also limits the tornado threat this evening. The mentioned\nbackdoor front should push the thunderstorm activity into SD\ntonight, although lingering instability will bring isolated\nthunderstorm chances through the night across the southeast.\nLows tonight will be in the 50s. There are some improved RH\nrecoveries tonight, although a steady northeast wind should\nlimit fog formation. Abundant wildfire smoke aloft will also be\nfound through tonight and again on Sunday. Patchy smoke may mix\nto the surface through this evening and again Sunday through\nSunday evening.\n\nSurface high lingers across the state Sunday morning, then gets\nnudge slightly northward. The stalled boundary to our south also\ngets nudge slightly northward and turns into a weak warm front.\nBy late Sunday afternoon and especially through Sunday night\nmodest CAPE and shear return along this boundary, with perhaps\nadditional help by a mid level wave in northwest flow. This \ncould bring another round of isolated to perhaps scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms. SPC has returned and expanded a \nMarginal Risk to much of southern ND for Sunday through Sunday \nnight. Hazards will be similar to tonights, with golf ball sized\nhail and winds to 60 mph. Storm mode may start out discrete \nthen go to more of a linear mode as storms progress along the \nboundary Sunday night. Otherwise look for more seasonable\ntemperatures Sunday and Sunday night with a steady east wind. \nIncreased humidity values from slightly cooler temperatures \nshould also improve the fire weather concerns for Sunday.\n\nZonal flow pattern will keep temperatures near normal to start\nnext week. Disturbances within this westerly flow could also\nprovide near daily chances for showers and thunderstorms. The\nunstable boundary found this weekend could linger as well\nperhaps bringing isolated strong to severe thunderstorms. SPC\nhas clipped some south central portions with a Marginal Risk on\nMonday as a result. CSU-MLP also showing this signal for Monday\nand again on Tuesday. Of note is WPC having a Marginal Risk for\nexcessive rainfall in southern and some western areas Monday\nthrough Wednesday, with the most widespread coverage on Tuesday.\nPwats are slightly increased on these days and will also depend\non the amount of thunderstorms that can develop in one area\nalong this boundary. Clusters then indicate either a continued\nzonal flow pattern or perhaps a shift to a weak broad trough\npattern could return mid to late week. This could result in\nfurther cooling of temperatures, with continued near daily\nchances for showers and thunderstorms.",
